  "version": "enrolled",
  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id) not stated.\n(b_no_id_remedy)\n(section) Section 48, KRS 117.228(1)\n(quote) STRUCK: [Except as provided in subsection (4) of this section, ]\n(section) Section 48, KRS 117.228(2)(a)\n(quote) STRUCK: [The voter's Social Security card; (b) ]\n(section) Section 48, KRS 117.228(2)(c)-(d)\n(quote) STRUCK: [; or (d) Any food stamp identification card, electronic benefit transfer card, or supplemental nutrition assistance card, that is issued by this state and has the name of the voter stated]\n(section) Section 48, KRS 117.228(4)-(5)\n(quote) STRUCK: [If the voter is personally known to the election officer, the election officer may execute an election officer affirmation, on a form prescribed and furnished by the State Board of Elections pursuant to administrative regulations promulgated under KRS Chapter 13A, affirming the voter's identification as being personally known to him or her.\n(section) Section 48, KRS 117.228(2)\n(quote) Any identification card issued by a county in this state which has the name of the voter stated and has been approved in writing by the State Board of Elections pursuant to administrative regulations promulgated under KRS Chapter 13A; or (b)[(c)] Any identification card with the voter's photograph and the name of the voter stated\n(c_mail_ballot_id) not stated.\n(d_proof_of_citizenship)\n(section) Section 30(1), new section of KRS Chapter 116\n(quote) The State Board of Elections is authorized to enter into agreements with agencies of the federal government to identify individuals who are deceased or who are not citizens of the United States but are registered to vote in this state.\n(section) Section 30(4)\n(quote) An individual identified as a noncitizen on the roster of qualified voters shall not cast a ballot until his or her United States citizenship is verified. To verify his or her United States citizenship, he or she shall present one (1) or more of the following at the office of his or her county clerk:\n(section) Section 30(4)(b)-(e)\n(quote) (b) An undamaged United States passport that is or was valid for ten (10) years for adults or five (5) years for children under sixteen (16) and does not contain a United States National endorsement; (c) A Consular Report of Birth Abroad or Certification of Birth; (d) A Certificate of Naturalization; or (e) A Certificate of Citizenship.\n(section) Section 30(6)\n(quote) If an individual identified as a noncitizen on the roster of qualified voters appears at a voting location to vote, the election officer shall allow the voter to cast a provisional ballot only after the voter has attested under penalty of perjury that he or she is a United States citizen on a form provided by the State Board of Elections.\n(section) Section 51\n(quote) Section 30 of this Act takes effect January 1, 2028.\n(e_other_rule)\n(section) Section 30(2)(b)1-2\n(quote) The federal agency shall be provided with a copy of the roster of all qualified registered voters within the state maintained by the State Board of Elections pursuant to KRS 117.025; 2. The copy of the roster that is provided to the federal agency shall include only the name, date of birth, and last four (4) digits of the Social Security number, if available, of registered voters within the state;\n(section) Section 30(3)(a)\n(quote) Mark the individual's registration in the roster of all qualified registered voters maintained pursuant to KRS 117.025(3)(a) in such a way that indicates on the voter registration system and any e-poll book that the individual has been identified as a noncitizen\n(section) Section 30(7)\n(quote) An individual identified as a noncitizen on the roster of qualified voters shall be purged from the roster of qualified voters if the individual does not verify his or her United States citizenship before the day after the date of the second general election for federal office that occurs after the individual was identified as a noncitizen on the roster of qualified voters.\n(section) Section 30(8)\n(quote) The voter registration of all individuals who are identified as being a noncitizen through an agreement authorized by this section shall be forwarded by the State Board of Elections to the Unit of Election Investigations and Security within the Office of the Attorney General.\n(section) Section 1, KRS 116.112(1)\n(quote) Intergovernmental agreements for the exchanging of any data shall be permitted if the[ sole] purpose of exchanging data is to remove ineligible voters.\n(section) Section 18, KRS 119.165(4)\n(quote) Any resident of this state who, by means other than falsely personating a registered voter, votes[ at a regular or special election before he has resided in this state thirty (30) days, or in the county and precinct where the election is held the time required by law, or before he has attained full age, or] before he or she has become a citizen, shall be guilty of a Class B misdemeanor.\n(other) Section 1 (KRS 116.112): State Board may make data-exchange agreements with other agencies for voter-list upkeep; the word 'sole' is struck before 'purpose'.\n(other) Section 2 (KRS 116.200): city/school boundary maps for county clerks; school-board boundary deadline moved from August 1 to June 1.\n(other) Sections 3, 8, 32-34 (KRS 117.125, 117.379, 117.155, 117.205, 117.383): voting-system standards (Election Assistance Commission), no barcodes/QR codes for tabulation, recount rule.\n(other) Sections 4-7, 9 (KRS 117.145, 117.265, 117.275, 117.355, 117.389): write-in supplies, write-in declaration deadline, signature-roster/result delivery within 10 days, equipment testing after petitions.\n(other) Sections 10-16, 21-23, 26-27 (KRS 118.x, 118A.x): definition of political party (and repeal of KRS 118.551 in Section 50), candidate filing forms and name form, presidential-primary conforming changes.\n(other) Sections 17-18 (KRS 119.015, 119.165): 'falsely' struck from the county-clerk registration offence; 119.165(4) keeps 'before he or she has become a citizen' (Class B misdemeanor) and strikes the 30-day / residency / age grounds.\n(other) Sections 19-20 (KRS 132.017, 160.210): school-tax petition election timing and bond; no school-division boundary change after June 1 in an election year.\n(other) Sections 24-25, 29, 35-37 (KRS 83A.170, 83A.175, 83A.040, 118.176, 118A new, 120.165): city officer removal / special election rules, candidate challenge costs, judicial-candidate party disclosure.\n(other) Sections 28, 31 (KRS 27A.070, 116.113): court lists of felony convictions and jury-duty citizenship excusals to the elections board.\n(other) Sections 38-47 (KRS 121.x): campaign-finance rules.\n(other) Section 49 (KRS 116.114): annual voter-roll cleanup report now lists 'citizenship status changes' among eligibility changes; agreements for exchanging data to remove ineligible voters expressly permitted.\n(other) Section 51: Section 30 takes effect January 1, 2028. Section 52: emergency clause; Sections 1 to 27, 32, 33, 49 and 50 take effect on approval.\n(other) version: orig_bill (as introduced, Sections 1-23): no ID text (slots a-d not stated). Slot e only: Section 18 amends KRS 119.165(4), keeping 'before he or she has become a citizen'. No Section 30 (noncitizen check) and no Section 48 (KRS 117.228) in this version.\n(other) version: 0175 (this file is the ENROLLED Act, 'CHAPTER 175 (HB 139)', Acts ch. 175, not a floor amendment as the skeleton's kind says): adds the new KRS 116 section (Section 30, federal-agreement noncitizen check, effective 2028-01-01 per Section 51) and Section 48 (KRS 117.228 strikes SS card, benefit card and personal-knowledge exception). Both carry through to enrolment unchanged.\n(other) version: bill (furthest = veto-override printing, header 26 RS HB 139/VO): same operative text as 0175. A word-by-word comparison finds only hyphenation line-break differences and the missing 'Veto Overridden' stamp.\n(other) version: HCS1 (House committee substitute 1, 26 RS HB 139/HCS 1, saved 2026-11-11): no ID or citizenship text in slots a-d (no noncitizen-check section, no KRS 117.228 section). Slot e only, as in the introduced bill: Section 18 reprints 'before he or she has become a citizen' in KRS 119.165(4), and Section 1 reprints the intergovernmental data-exchange sentence in KRS 116.112. Against the introduced bill it adds one section (KRS 118.105, nomination of candidates) and renumbers.\n(other) version: HFA1 (House floor amendment 1, saved 2026-11-11): no ID or citizenship text. It adds Sections 25 to 28 amending KRS 83A.170, 83A.175, 118.212 and 118A.150 (nonpartisan city primaries and judicial candidates). Adopted with HCS 1.\n(other) version: SCS1 (Senate committee substitute 1, 26 RS HB 139/SCS 1, saved 2026-11-11): carries the ID and citizenship text. Against HCS 1 it adds the new KRS 116 section (Section 30, federal-agreement check for noncitizens and the document step for flagged voters), Section 48 (KRS 117.228 strikes the Social Security card item, the benefit-card item and the personal-knowledge subsection), a narrowed KRS 116.112 data-exchange sentence, and about twenty other sections (campaign finance in KRS 121, KRS 27A.070, 83A.040, 116.113 and more). The enrolled Act (0175) and the veto-override printing ('bill') have the same operative text as SCS 1 apart from the Acts stamp and hyphenation.\n(other) note: Enacted Acts ch. 175 (2026). The noncitizen-check section (Section 30) takes effect 2028-01-01; Sections 1-27, 32, 33, 49, 50 take effect on approval under the emergency clause; the saved text does not state an effective date for Section 48 (KRS 117.228). Section 48 also strikes the cross-reference 'Except as provided in subsection (4)' and the whole personal-knowledge subsection (4), so an election officer can no longer vouch for a voter without ID; the voter's signed affirmation (which still includes 'The voter is a citizen of the United States') plus a county ID card or photo ID card is what remains. 'Proof of identification' itself is only referenced, so slot a is not stated. Section 30 names no database; it works through federal-agency agreements, and only people the check flags must show a listed document (at the county clerk's office only, unlike HB534's House text). The voted House 2/19 and Senate versions are not saved.",
